# Writing

Short sentences. One idea each. Say what the thing does.

Use this for markdown, a pull request body, and a commit message. It is not an always-on code rule.

1. Sentence-case headings.
2. Plain verbs. Name the actor when you know it.
3. Define a term the first time. Then use that word.
4. Drop filler. That includes just, simply, really, basically, actually, very, "in order to", and "it is important to note".
5. No em dashes. Use a period or a comma.
6. A colon introduces a list. It does not join two sentences.
7. Do not force three examples. Use the number you have.
8. Do not cycle synonyms for the same thing.
9. No chatbot phrases, and no praise for the question.
10. Prefer the plain word. "Use", not "utilize".
11. A commit subject is `type(scope): description`. Imperative. No trailing period.
12. A pull request body uses the sections in `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`. A few short sentences. No wall of identifiers.
13. No secrets, customer names, or home-directory paths.
14. Cut a sentence that repeats the one before it.
15. If a sentence could sit unchanged in another project's docs, it does not say anything about this one. Cut it or name the mechanism.

